contrib/pg_bp_car/README

pg_bp_car -- Clock with Adaptive Replacement (CAR)
===================================================

pg_bp_car provides the CAR buffer replacement algorithm as a PostgreSQL
extension.  CAR delivers the same adaptive caching behavior as ARC but
replaces ARC's per-hit spinlock with an atomic reference-bit write,
making it significantly more scalable on many-core systems.


Installation
============

    CREATE EXTENSION pg_bp_car;


Quick Start
===========

    CREATE BUFFER POOL my_car_pool HANDLER car_pool_handler SIZE '128MB';
    CREATE TABLE hot_data (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'my_car_pool');

Tables, indexes, and materialized views can be assigned to a CAR pool.
The pool can also be set as the default algorithm at the server level.


When to Choose CAR Over ARC
============================

Choose CAR when:

  - You have high-concurrency OLTP on many cores (32+).  ARC acquires a
    spinlock on every buffer hit to move entries between lists.  CAR
    only sets a reference bit, eliminating that contention entirely.

  - Your workload has a high cache hit ratio.  The higher the hit rate,
    the more often ARC's spinlock fires relative to useful I/O.  CAR's
    lock-free hit path keeps overhead proportional to actual misses.

  - You want prefetch-aware caching.  CAR implements a prefetch_hint
    callback that checks ghost lists when upcoming pages are signaled,
    allowing the adaptive target to shift before real accesses arrive.

  - You want T1-priority background writing.  CAR's trickle writer
    preferentially flushes T1 (recency) pages that are most likely to
    be evicted soon, reducing eviction I/O latency.

Choose ARC when:

  - Precise LRU ordering matters more than hit-path concurrency.  ARC
    keeps an explicit ordered list; CAR approximates recency with a
    binary reference bit.

  - Your system has few cores and low concurrency, where spinlock
    contention is not a bottleneck and ARC's simpler code path may be
    marginally faster.


Algorithm Overview
==================

CAR uses four data structures:

    T1 -- clock ring with reference bits (tracks recency)
    T2 -- clock ring with reference bits (tracks frequency)
    B1 -- ghost list for pages evicted from T1
    B2 -- ghost list for pages evicted from T2

On a cache hit, CAR sets the reference bit on the entry.  No lock is
acquired, no list manipulation occurs.  T1-to-T2 promotion happens
lazily: when the clock hand encounters a T1 entry with its reference
bit set, it clears the bit and promotes the entry to T2.

On a cache miss, CAR checks the ghost lists (B1/B2).  A hit on B1
increases target_T1_size (favor recency); a hit on B2 decreases it
(favor frequency).  This is the same adaptation rule as ARC.

On eviction, the clock hand sweeps T1 first: entries with a set
reference bit are promoted to T2; entries with a clear reference bit
and no pin are evicted.  If T1 does not yield a victim, T2 is swept
similarly.


VACUUM Integration
==================

When VACUUM loads pages into the pool, they are inserted with their
reference bit cleared.  This makes VACUUM-loaded pages the first
candidates for eviction during clock sweep, preventing bulk maintenance
operations from polluting the cache.


Statistics
==========

    SELECT * FROM pg_stat_car;

Columns:

    name             -- pool name
    oid              -- pool OID (NULL for built-in pools)
    t1_size          -- current number of resident T1 entries
    t2_size          -- current number of resident T2 entries
    b1_size          -- current number of B1 ghost entries
    b2_size          -- current number of B2 ghost entries
    target_t1_size   -- adaptive T1 target size
    t1_hand          -- CDB index of T1 clock hand (-1 if empty)
    t2_hand          -- CDB index of T2 clock hand (-1 if empty)
    lookups          -- total buffer lookups
    t1_hits          -- hits in T1 (recency ring)
    t2_hits          -- hits in T2 (frequency ring)
    b1_hits          -- ghost hits in B1 (drives target_t1_size up)
    b2_hits          -- ghost hits in B2 (drives target_t1_size down)
    misses           -- complete cache misses (not in T1/T2/B1/B2)
    t1_evictions     -- pages evicted from T1
    t2_evictions     -- pages evicted from T2


Size Advisory
=============

    SELECT * FROM pg_bp_car_size_recommendation('my_car_pool')
        AS (current_size int, recommended_size int,
            ghost_pressure float8, hit_ratio float8);

    current_size      -- current pool size in buffers
    recommended_size  -- suggested pool size (up to 2x current)
    ghost_pressure    -- ratio of ghost hits to misses; values above
                         0.1 indicate the pool would benefit from more
                         memory
    hit_ratio         -- fraction of lookups served from T1 or T2


Limitations
===========

Clock sweep O(n) worst case.  If all reference bits in a ring are set,
the clock hand must traverse the entire ring before finding a victim.
In practice this is rare because the sweep clears bits as it goes.

Reference bit is coarser than explicit LRU ordering.  ARC's explicit
list ordering provides continuous recency information.  CAR's binary
reference bit is a coarser approximation (hot vs. cold, rather than a
full ordering).

Spinlock still needed on the miss path.  Ghost list lookup and
target_T1_size adaptation require a spinlock, so misses are not
lock-free.  This is acceptable because misses are far less frequent
than hits.

Memory overhead: 2x CDB entries per buffer.  Same as ARC.  Each
CarCDB is slightly larger than ArcCDB due to the additional
clock_prev, clock_next, ghost_prev, and ghost_list_next fields.


Patent Status
=============

CAR is NOT covered by IBM's ARC patent (US 6,996,676).  The CAR
algorithm was specifically designed by Bansal and Modha as a
patent-free alternative to ARC with comparable adaptive performance.


References
==========

S. Bansal and D. Modha, "CAR: Clock with Adaptive Replacement",
Proceedings of USENIX FAST 2004.
https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast-04/car-clock-adaptive-replacement


Patent disposition
==================

CAR's published reference is the IBM patent application
US20070106846A1 (https://patents.google.com/patent/US20070106846A1/en)
and US 7,058,766 in the same family.  The 20-year statutory term on
the priority filing has now elapsed; the algorithm and its scan-
resistant variant CART have entered the public domain.

This extension is included on the understanding that the patents
have expired; downstreams should still confirm with their own
counsel before relying on that conclusion in their jurisdiction.

References
